Name: playRate

Type: property

Syntax: set the playRate of <player> to <rate>

Syntax: set the playRate of <image> to <rate>

Summary:
Specifies how fast a <player> plays a movie, or how fast an <animated
GIF> <image> animates.

Associations: player, image

Introduced: 1.0

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop, server

Example:
set the playRate of player 17 to 0.5 -- half-speed

Example:
set the playRate of image "Demon" to 2 -- the sprite walks twice as fast

Value:
The <playRate> of a <player> is a <real number>.
By default, the <playRate> <property> of a newly created <player> is set
to 1.

Description:
Use the <playRate> <property> to speed up or slow down playback of a
movie or sound.

The <playRate> is the ratio between the desired playback rate and the
natural rate of the movie or sound. A <playRate> of 1 plays the movie or
sound at normal speed; a <playRate> greater than one speeds up the
playback; a <playRate> less than one slows down the playback.

A negative <playRate> plays the movie or sound backward.

If the <playRate> is zero, the movie or sound is paused.

**Images:** the <playRate> of an <image> sets how fast an <animated GIF>
animates: 1 (the default) is the pace of its frames' own delays, 2 twice
as fast, 0.5 half as fast. Each frame's duration (its GIF delay, or what
the <frameDurations> of the <image> says) is divided by the <playRate>
and rounded to the nearest millisecond, when that frame comes up. The
<playRate> of an <image> must be greater than 0: use the <repeatCount> to
stop an animation, and the <currentFrame> to step through it. Like the
<frameDurations>, it is not saved with the stack and is not one of the
<properties> of an <image>; a <clone> or copy of the <image> has the same
setting. The <playRate> of an <image> was added in version 9.7.1
(OXT-Beyond 0.2.8-dp.2).

